%
\newcommand{\thesisabstract}{
\begin{center}
\MakeUppercase{\thesistitle}
 
\vspace{12pt}
ABSTRACT
\vspace{12pt}
\end{center}
Long-lived language model agents accumulate memories that go stale. A fact true when written is later superseded, but retrieval keeps surfacing the old value, and the agent answers from a fact that no longer holds. Research on agent memory has concentrated on storage and retrieval; the rule deciding what memory discards has been treated as an implementation detail rather than as an object of study.

This thesis isolates that rule. DRIFT holds a bitemporal knowledge graph substrate, the fact extraction step, the retrieval function, the answer model, and the evaluation judge fixed, and varies only the forgetting policy across seven conditions spanning a no-memory floor, an append-only baseline, and five forgetting strategies drawn from current systems. The framework is evaluated on two public benchmarks that annotate the superseded answer, an annotation the staleness metric requires and that the two most widely used agent-memory benchmarks do not provide.

Four findings replicate across both benchmarks. Append-only memory measurably surfaces superseded facts, with staleness confidence intervals excluding zero. Bitemporal contradiction invalidation is statistically equivalent to the accuracy ceiling, bounded within roughly two to two and a half points on both benchmarks, while spending twelve percent fewer tokens per question and storing six and a half to fifteen percent fewer facts. Novelty-gated writing, a popular efficiency technique, increases staleness by eleven to thirteen points, because a correction is textually near-identical to the fact it corrects and is rejected as a duplicate, so it never enters memory. Structure-aware but time-blind consolidation performs significantly worse than bitemporal consolidation on real-world knowledge updates, attributing the advantage to bitemporal semantics specifically rather than to graph structure in general.

Two methodological results accompany these findings: a configuration ablation quantifying how much of the reported efficiency depends on correctly declaring a domain's functional predicates, and a judge-reliability audit identifying a twelve-and-a-half-point accuracy artifact produced by a rotating judge pool on byte-identical answers.

The framework is released as a dependency-free, offline-verifiable Python package with a five-line integration surface, so the central question, which forgetting policy suits a given workload, can be answered empirically rather than assumed.
\vspace{12pt}

